Original commit body from e1beb4e932b017051fffb1b560bdac691e8bdab7:

WI-duson. AGENTS.md says two things, each correct alone: "during
development, run only relevant tests" and "commit .ci/affected-tests.txt
with every PR". Explicit mode overwrote the manifest with a
zero-selection stub, so following both shipped a manifest selecting
NOTHING -- and the live Woodpecker gate keys on "did the manifest select
any tests?", so it printed "manifest selects no tests - skipping pytest"
and reported green.

An explicit run is not a selection computation. It was never asked about
the diff, and the honest thing for a command with no answer is to say
nothing rather than publish an empty one. The committed artifact is left
exactly as it was, and the run says so rather than passing silently.

MEASURED AS A PAIR, same command and same tree, the pre-fix script run
from inside the repo:

    before   manifest replaced with "# Mode: explicit", 0 selected tests
    after    manifest byte-identical (sha256 unchanged)

THE GUARD ASSERTS THE ONE GOOD MODE NOW. .githooks/pre-commit rejected
exactly `Mode: full-suite` and passed everything else through its else arm
with a GREEN tick -- so the zero-selection manifest committed cleanly past
the very check that exists to protect that file. full-suite runs too much
and explicit runs nothing; only the first was guarded. It now requires
`Mode: targeted`, with a distinct reason per rejection so the message
sends the reader to the right fix, and the test extracts the check and
drives it against targeted / full-suite / explicit / an unrecognised mode.

That test's own slice was wrong twice before it was right: an unindented
"\nfi\n" search ran past the nested check to the hook's outermost fi, and
the balance check meant to catch that counted the substring "fi" out of
the hook's prose. It asks bash -n whether the slice is a program now.

